\section{PermG FPR Independence Analysis}
\label{app:fpr_independence}

Table~\ref{tab:null_fpr_methods} reports null-control FPR for all five detection methods across six seeds ($6 \times 6 = 36$ pairs, 6-pair basis excluding mauve).
Under identical BH-FDR correction ($q{=}0.05$), DiffXCorr achieves the lowest FPR (1/36, 2.8\%) but lacks directional separation; PermG (4/36, 11.1\%) is the lowest among directional methods.
All four PermG false positives occur in token\_entropy$\to$distinct pairs (seeds~45 and 50, forward direction only), suggesting a structural pattern concentrated in a single canary--downstream pair type.
The 8 canary$\to$downstream pairs within each seed share canary terms (token entropy, ECE), inducing positive regression dependence on a subset (PRDS).
BH-FDR correction remains valid under PRDS~\cite{benjamini2001control}, so the shared-training-data dependence structure does not invalidate our multiple testing correction.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Null-control FPR by method ($\alpha{=}0.0$, 6~seeds 45--50, 6~pairs each). All p-value--based methods (DiffXCorr, PermG, TY) report BH-FDR--corrected rejections at $q{=}0.05$ for fair comparison. Threshold uses a deterministic $3\sigma$ criterion; NaiveXCorr uses uncorrected correlation significance.}
\label{tab:null_fpr_methods}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Method & Criterion & Rejections (/36) & FPR (\%) \\
\midrule
Diff.\ XCorr$^\dagger$ & BH-FDR & 1 & 2.8 \\
\textbf{Perm.\ Granger} & \textbf{BH-FDR} & \textbf{4} & \textbf{11.1} \\
Threshold & $3\sigma$ (det.) & 10 & 27.8 \\
Toda--Yamamoto & BH-FDR & 14 & 38.9 \\
Naive XCorr & uncorr. & 33 & 91.7 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Bidirectional; cannot separate forward from reverse.} \\
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_null_fpr_diagnostic.pdf}
\caption{Null-control FPR by method ($\alpha{=}0.0$, 6~seeds, 6-pair basis). Bars: pooled FPR (rejections/36); dots: per-seed FPR. PermG (11.1\%) is the lowest-FPR directional method; DiffXCorr (2.8\%) is lowest overall but lacks forward/reverse separation.}
\label{fig:null_fpr_diagnostic}
\end{subfigure}
\\[1ex]
\begin{subfigure}[t]{\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_bootstrap_fpr.pdf}
\caption{Bootstrap distribution of PermG FPR (10{,}000 resamples, 6 null seeds). Mean FPR $= 11.1\%$ (red line); bootstrap percentile 95\% CI $[0.0\%, 22.2\%]$ (orange band). The Clopper--Pearson cluster-adjusted CI ($[1.2\%, 30.4\%]$) is wider due to ICC correction.}
\label{fig:bootstrap_fpr}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{PermG null-control false positive analysis. (a)~Per-method FPR comparison. (b)~Bootstrap FPR distribution.}
\label{fig:fpr_combined}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Cluster adjustment.}
The 36 null-control pairs are nested within 6~seeds (6~pairs per seed), introducing within-seed correlation.
Within-seed ICC${=}0.14$ yields an effective sample size $N_{\text{eff}} \approx 21$ (from $N_{\text{eff}} = N / [1 + (m-1) \cdot \text{ICC}]$ where $N{=}36$, $m{=}6$).
Under the Clopper--Pearson exact method with $N_{\text{eff}}{=}21$ and 4 successes (scaled), the cluster-adjusted 95\% CI is $[1.2\%, 30.4\%]$, compared to $[3.1\%, 26.1\%]$ under independence ($N{=}36$).
The lower ICC compared to the 3-seed pilot ($0.14$ vs.\ $0.34$) reflects greater between-seed consistency with six independent replications.

\paragraph{Direction filtering.}
Forward-direction filtering (retaining only pairs with peak lag${<}0$ for cross-correlation methods) reduces null-control $K{\geq}3$ false positives.
Under the 6-seed, 6-pair basis, $K{\geq}3$ consensus with filtering produces 6/36 false positives (16.7\%; detail below); the 3-seed pilot underestimated this rate (0/8).
Detection at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$ is unaffected: $K{\geq}3$ counts are identical with and without direction filtering at all signal levels.

\paragraph{$K{\geq}3$ false positive detail.}
Under the 6-pair basis (6~seeds, 36~pairs), $K{\geq}3$ consensus produces 6 false positives (FPR${=}16.7\%$).
Of these, 4 are driven by naive cross-correlation, threshold onset, and Toda--Yamamoto (PermG does \emph{not} vote); the remaining 2 (seeds~45 and 50, te$\to$distinct) include PermG in the consensus.
PermG thus contributes to only 2 of 6 consensus false positives, confirming that most $K{\geq}3$ FPR is driven by the high-FPR methods (naive, threshold, TY).

\paragraph{Sensitivity to FPR assumption.}
Even under the conservative upper bound of the cluster-adjusted CI (30.4\%), PermG remains the lowest-FPR directional method: DiffXCorr achieves a lower FPR under identical BH-FDR correction (2.8\%) but lacks forward/reverse separation; the next directional method (threshold onset) has 27.8\% and Toda--Yamamoto 38.9\%.
Our primary results rely on equal-weight $K{\geq}3$ majority voting, where each method contributes exactly one vote regardless of its FPR---this mechanism is completely unaffected by any FPR assumption.
For the exploratory FPR-inverse weighting, PermG's weight ($w{=}1/0.111{=}9.0$) exceeds all directional alternatives (threshold: 3.6, TY: 2.6, naive: 1.1); DiffXCorr ($w{=}1/0.028{=}35.7$) receives a higher weight but lacks directional separation, preserving PermG's rank among directional methods.
The insensitivity of both the voting mechanism and the method ranking to the upper CI bound provides evidence that the MMCT conclusions are robust to uncertainty in PermG's Type~I error rate.

\paragraph{$T{=}15$ validation.}
To verify that FPR control holds under longer observation windows, we repeat the null experiment at $T{=}15$ (2~seeds, 6-pair basis, 12~pairs total).
PermG produces 0/12 false positives (0\%), compared to 4/36 (11.1\%) at $T{=}10$.
$K{\geq}3$ consensus yields 1/12 (8.3\%; seed~43, ece$\to$distinct\_1, driven by naive+diff+threshold votes without PermG participation).
The absence of PermG false positives at $T{=}15$ is consistent with the power-threshold interpretation of $\alpha_c$: longer sequences increase statistical power for true signals without inflating the null rejection rate, supporting the prediction that $\alpha_c$ left-shifts with $T$ while FPR remains controlled.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our landscape characterization uses Pythia-410M as the primary architecture; the phase diagram is Pythia-family-only, frontier-scale behavior (${>}$7B) is untested, and cross-architecture evaluation is limited to $\alpha{=}1.0$.
PermG's empirical FPR is 7.4\% (CI $[2.1\%, 17.9\%]$) and $K{\geq}3$ consensus yields 11.1\% (6/54); per-condition detection rates carry wide binomial CIs (e.g., $[0.25, 0.92]$ for 6/8), so the phase diagram represents a coarse topology rather than precise probability estimates.
All experiments use full replacement with a single decoding strategy (temperature~$1.0$, top-$k{=}50$); data accumulation~\cite{dey2024breaking} or lower temperatures would shift the landscape.
Permutation Granger testing establishes temporal precedence, not causation; our short-sequence regime ($T{\leq}25$) limits statistical power relative to econometric standards ($T{\geq}100$), and nonlinear alternatives to the linear PermG test yield no significant results in our setting (see \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We study the detectability landscape of iterative training collapse using Pythia-410M~\cite{biderman2023pythia} (410M parameters, decoder-only) as the primary architecture, with cross-architecture validation on five families (later verified in \S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Training data is drawn from WikiText-103~\cite{merity2017pointer} following the replace paradigm of~\citet{shumailov2024curse}: each generation fine-tunes on a mixture of $\alpha$ synthetic and $(1{-}\alpha)$ real data, generates 5{,}000 replacement samples, and fully replaces the synthetic pool (hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{app:training_details}; notation in Appendix~\ref{app:background}).
We run $T{=}10$ generations across 13 contamination levels ($\alpha \in \{0.00, 0.05, 0.10, 0.25, 0.30, 0.35, 0.40, 0.45, 0.50, 0.55, 0.60, 0.75, 1.00\}$, including null control) with multi-seed replication (4 seeds at $\alpha{=}1.0$; 3 at primary levels).
In total, the study comprises ${\sim}192$ iterative training runs across two model scales (410M, 1.4B), five detection methods, 13 contamination levels, three sequence lengths ($T \in \{10, 15, 20\}$), and up to four random seeds per condition, yielding ${\sim}1{,}500$ statistical tests after BH-FDR correction.

We track two \emph{canary metrics} (token entropy and ECE) and three \emph{downstream metrics}: distinct-1, distinct-2~\cite{li2016diversity}, and perplexity (distinct-3: Appendix~\ref{app:distinct3}).
All hypothesis tests apply BH-FDR correction~\cite{benjamini1995controlling} at $q{=}0.05$; PermG uses 5{,}000 permutations on first-differenced series~\cite{shojaie2022granger} with maximum lag~5 (window sensitivity: Appendix~\ref{app:window_sensitivity}).
As in \S\ref{sec:method}, PermG tests temporal precedence, not causation.


\subsection{Sharp Detection Onset}
\label{sec:regime}

Diversity collapse occurs at every contamination level: distinct-1 declines by $14.1\%$ even at $\alpha{=}0.0$ (fine-tuning drift) and by $40.3{\pm}0.3\%$ at $\alpha{=}0.25$ (3-seed mean).
Despite this ubiquitous degradation, Granger detectability is sharply regime-dependent.
PermG finds 0/8 canary$\to$downstream pairs FDR-significant from $\alpha{=}0.00$ through $\alpha{=}0.45$, then jumps to 6/8 (Clopper--Pearson 95\% CI $[0.25, 0.92]$) at $\alpha{=}0.50$, uniformly across all 3~fp32 seeds.

This transition is striking because cumulative synthetic fraction barely changes across the boundary: $C = 1{-}(1{-}\alpha)^{10}$ yields $99.75\%$ at $\alpha{=}0.45$ versus $99.90\%$ at $\alpha{=}0.50$, a difference of 0.15 percentage points, yet detection jumps from null to reliable.
The onset reflects PermG's statistical power crossing the detection threshold, not the emergence of a new physical signal; sub-threshold $p$-values at $\alpha{=}0.45$ ($p_{\mathrm{fdr}} = 0.059$--$0.091$) confirm proximity to the boundary (\S\ref{sec:theory}).
Sigmoid regression on the 13-point $T{=}10$ grid yields $\alpha_c = 0.483 \pm 0.006$ ($R^2 = 0.969$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}); dense $\alpha$ sampling (17~values) constrains onset steepness to $k{=}15.7$ (95\% CI $[9.4, 28.2]$), a steep but finite-width sigmoid ($\Delta\mathrm{AIC}{=}-110$ vs.\ step function; Appendix~\ref{app:sigmoid_shape}).
This steepness (${\approx}3{\times}$ smooth-model predictions; \S\ref{sec:theory}) and cross-seed consistency (3/3 seeds: $0/8{\to}6/8$) argue for a genuine, power-modulated transition rather than a statistical artifact (Fisher exact: $p = 3.45 \times 10^{-11}$; specification-invariance across 1{,}160 configurations in Appendix~\ref{app:spec_curve}).

\paragraph{Canary scoping.}
First-differencing scopes the canary claim precisely.
Canary$\to$diversity pairs retain unanimous FDR significance after first-differencing ($24/24 \to 24/24$ across 4~seeds at $\alpha{=}1.0$, 6 per seed), while canary$\to$perplexity collapses entirely ($F \approx 0$, $p = 0.99$): the apparent perplexity association is a shared-trend artifact~\cite{granger1974spurious}.
All canary claims are therefore scoped to diversity metrics only.
This scoping reflects a genuine temporal structure difference: canary$\to$diversity correlations peak at lagged offsets (81\% at lag${<}0$), while canary$\to$perplexity correlations are contemporaneous (Appendix~\ref{app:channel_effect_size}).
At $\alpha{=}1.0$, all 24 canary$\to$diversity pairs reach FDR significance in the forward direction with 0/24 reverse; entropy onset at generation~1 precedes distinct-$n$ at generation~2 in 16/18 WikiText conditions (\S\ref{sec:cascade}, Appendix~\ref{app:moment_cascade}).

\paragraph{Blind zone.}
The canary mechanism has a fundamental limitation: at $\alpha{=}0.10$, distinct-1 has already dropped by $39.6\%$, yet token entropy is indistinguishable from the null ($-5.3\%$ versus $-4.6{\pm}0.4\%$ at $\alpha{=}0.0$).
The gen-1 entropy deviation spans three orders of magnitude across the $\alpha$ range ($0.5\sigma$ at $\alpha{=}0.10$ to $60\sigma$ at $\alpha{=}0.75$), explaining why the blind zone persists despite increasing contamination.\footnote{Seed~43 at $\alpha{=}0.10$ shows 3/8 versus 0/8 for seed~42; no other sub-threshold level exhibits seed-dependent detection.}
Below $\alpha_c$ at $T{=}10$, diversity collapse proceeds without any detectable Granger-precedence signal, though Gaussian MI remains positive (Appendix~\ref{app:mi_analysis}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/metric_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Metric trajectories at $\alpha{=}1.0$ (Pythia-410M, 4~seeds). Token entropy drops at generation~1; distinct-1 declines from generations~2--3, with a consistent one-generation canary lead across all four seeds (shaded: inter-seed range). This pattern corresponds to 24/24 forward and 0/24 reverse FDR-significant canary$\to$diversity pairs (6 per seed, mauve excluded).}
\label{fig:metric_trajectory}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Dose-Response and Detection Regimes}
\label{sec:dose_response}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth,trim={0 0.4cm 0 0.4cm},clip]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\vspace{-6pt}
\caption{PermG dose-response profile (Pythia-410M, $T{=}10$, fp32). Sigmoid fit (empirical trend; shaded 95\% CI) yields $\alpha_c{=}0.483$. Three confirmed regimes: blind ($\alpha{\leq}0.45$, 0/8), onset ($\alpha{\approx}0.50$, 6/8), detection ($\alpha{\geq}0.75$); non-monotonic anomaly at $\alpha{=}0.60$ (0/8) (out of 8 canary$\to$downstream pairs). The sigmoid is a descriptive fit to observed detection rates, not a predictive model.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

The PermG dose-response profile (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}; full per-method table in Appendix~\ref{app:dose_response}) reveals three confirmed detection regimes---\emph{blind} ($\alpha \leq 0.45$, 0/8 FDR-significant pairs), \emph{onset} ($\alpha \approx 0.50$, 6/8), and \emph{detection} ($\alpha{\geq}0.75$, 6/8 across 4~seeds at $\alpha{=}1.0$)---plus a non-monotonic anomaly at $\alpha \approx 0.60$ (0/8) whose mechanism remains unexplained.
At $\alpha{=}0.55$, PermG returns 0/8 FDR-significant pairs despite marginal $p$-values ($p_{\mathrm{fdr}}{=}0.062$--$0.092$); differenced cross-correlation detects 8/8 forward pairs at this level (0/8 reverse), confirming that a lead-lag signal exists but falls below PermG's directional detection threshold.
At $\alpha{=}0.60$, PermG returns $0/8$ at $T{\leq}20$ but recovers to $5/8$ at $T{=}25$, consistent with $\sqrt{T}$ power scaling (Appendix~\ref{app:t20_comparison}).
Notably, $K{\geq}3$ consensus also returns 0/8 at $\alpha{=}0.60$: all direction-checked methods fail at this level (PermG 0/8, threshold onset 0/8, Toda--Yamamoto 1/8), while bidirectional DiffXCorr maintains detection (6/8 forward).
This suggests the anomaly reflects compressed temporal lag---canary and downstream respond within the same generation---disrupting all methods that require directional separation.

Method choice is itself a major source of variability: at $\alpha{=}1.0$, direction-checked forward significance spans 0\% to 75\% (Figure~\ref{fig:method_comparison}), with $\kappa{=}0.185$; a specification curve (18~variants; Appendix~\ref{app:spec_curve}) confirms onset invariance.
Task degradation corroborates: perplexity jumps $+31\%$ between $\alpha{=}0.50$ and $0.52$ (Appendix~\ref{app:task_degradation}).


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_method_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Method sensitivity: FDR-significant forward pairs (\%) across contamination levels (Pythia-410M, seed~42, fp32). PermG (dark blue) achieves 0\% null-control FPR with stable 75\% detection at $\alpha \geq 0.50$. Dashed line: 5\% nominal. Full table in Appendix~\ref{app:dose_response}.}
\label{fig:method_comparison}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Detection Boundary Left-Shift}
\label{sec:t20}

Longer observation windows push the detection boundary to lower contamination levels: $\alpha_c(5){\geq}1.0$, $\alpha_c(10){=}0.487$, consistent with power-law scaling (\S\ref{sec:theory}).
The $\alpha{=}0.45$ column validates this left-shift: undetectable at $T{\leq}10$ (0/8), it crosses into reliable detection at $T{=}15$ (6/8, dual-seed) and remains there at $T{=}20$ and $T{=}25$.
Cross-seed replication shows PermG within ${\pm}1$, though boundary cells exhibit sensitivity ($[0, 6]/8$ at $T{=}10$, $\alpha{=}0.50$).
The $\alpha{=}0.60$ anomaly dissolves by $T{=}25$ ($5/8$), consistent with $\sqrt{T}$ power scaling; predict-then-verify yields 2/4 boundary predictions within CI (Appendix~\ref{app:t20_prediction_timeline}).


\subsection{Cross-Architecture Validation}
\label{sec:generalization}

To test whether the phase-diagram structure is Pythia-specific, we evaluate PermG at $\alpha{=}1.0$ across 15 model-seed combinations spanning five families, scales 345M--6.9B, and two domains (Table~\ref{tab:generalization}).
Forward detection reaches ${\geq}6/8$ in 11/15 conditions, confirming that PermG generalizes to architectures satisfying the monotonic entropy decline assumption (Principle~\ref{thm:entropy_leads}).
Domain matters: C4 yields the strongest detection (Pythia-1.4B: 2/8 WikiText $\to$ 8/8 C4), suggesting that domain-specific mode structure affects detectability.
The four non-confirming conditions each isolate a distinct failure mode: precision sensitivity (GPT-2 bf16: 0/8 vs fp32: 6/8), bidirectional confounding (GPT-2 s43), seed dependence (Pythia-1.4B: s42 2/8, s43 6/8), and entropy decoupling (SmolLM3-3B: 3/8, whose non-monotonic entropy violates Principle~\ref{thm:entropy_leads}).

\paragraph{FPR caveat.}
GPT-2-medium null-control (s42) yields 6/6 PermG forward false positives, an architecture-specific FPR inflation likely driven by its non-standard weight initialization.
While $K{\geq}3$ consensus returns 0/6 on GPT-2 null specifically, its overall FPR on Pythia-410M is 11.1\% (6/54; \S\ref{sec:mmct})---higher than standalone PermG---so consensus serves as a robustness mechanism against single-method anomalies rather than a general FPR safety net.
Standalone PermG claims require architecture-specific null calibration.

Full results are in Table~\ref{tab:generalization} (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_arch_table}).


\subsection{Canary-Based Intervention (Proof of Concept)}
\label{sec:intervention}

As an illustrative proof-of-concept under favorable conditions ($\alpha{=}1.0$, full replacement), we implement predictive cessation: training switches to pure real data when token entropy drops ${>}15\%$ from generation~0.
The 15\% threshold was selected post-hoc (robust band $[10\%, 22\%]$, zero false alarms; Appendix~\ref{app:intervention_sensitivity}).
The predictive trigger fires at generation~1 (one generation before a reactive distinct-1 baseline), reducing peak perplexity by 64\% ($56.99$ vs.\ $157.09$; Figure~\ref{fig:intervention}) in both Pythia-410M and 1.4B.
This single-setting demonstration ($N{=}2$ architectures, $\alpha{=}1.0$, post-hoc threshold) is not rigorously evaluated (CUSUM comparison: Appendix~\ref{app:csmp}).

